# KNOWN GAP — MEMORY UNSAFE, BOTH BACKENDS. `auto f = bump` (an INFERRED local
# bound to a named `&`-param function) loses the borrow entirely: the callable's
# declared signature is never registered, so the argument loop takes the legacy
# fallback and passes the struct/scalar BY VALUE where the callee expects a
# pointer.
#
# ⚠ DISTINCT FROM THE ANNOTATED-LOCAL CELL, and the discriminator is the GIR,
# not the symptom. For `Callable[void(P &)] f = bump` the GIR passes a real
# pointer (`_7 = borrow_mut _4`) and only the ABI TAG was wrong — fixed by
# writing the tag from the callee's declared ownership. For `auto f = bump` the
# GIR is `_1 = const @bump` with `_1: unit` and the call is
# `call @__callable_1(copy _1, copy _3)` — a VALUE copy of a struct TEMP. No ABI
# tag can repair that: the pointer is never formed, and the argument is not even
# the user's variable. Verify:
#     gg build --emit-gir tests/fixtures/known_gaps/callable_amp_auto_local_lost_write.gg
#
# The fix belongs at the `auto`-declaration site — the callable's declared
# signature must reach `callable_param_types` / `callable_param_ownerships` for
# an inferred binding exactly as it does for an annotated one — after which the
# arg loop takes the `lower_call_arg` path and the ABI write site already
# handles the rest. Filed in TODO.md.
#
# ⚠ IT KILLS THE `int &` CELL TOO, which the annotated-local defect did not:
# the scalar cell was accidentally correct there because a pointer WAS formed.
# Here nothing is.
#
# RED-VERIFIED at HEAD (with the indirect-call ABI write site landed):
#     C    -> rc 139 (SIGSEGV)
#     LLVM -> rc 139 (SIGSEGV)
#     self-host driver -> rc 139
#
# INTENDED: `2` then `2`.
struct P:
    int n

void bump(P &p):
    p.n = p.n + 1

void bump_int(int &n):
    n = n + 1

int main():
    auto f = bump
    P p = P(1)
    f(&p)
    print(p.n)
    auto g = bump_int
    int k = 1
    g(&k)
    print(k)
    return 0
